{% table %}
- Section
- What it does
- Length
---
- Job title
- Gets found in search and sets the level
- 3–6 words
---
- Summary
- Explains the role and why it matters
- 2–3 sentences
---
- What you'll do
- Shows the actual work
- 4–6 bullets
---
- Requirements
- Sets the true bar
- 3–6 bullets
---
- Nice to have
- Signals bonus skills without scaring people off
- 2–4 bullets
---
- Tech stack
- Lets engineers self-select
- One line or short list
---
- Compensation
- Saves everyone time
- A range, plus equity if offered
---
- Location and work model
- Remote, hybrid or office, and time zones
- 1–2 lines
---
- Interview process
- Sets expectations
- 3–5 steps
---
- About the company
- Stage, product, team size
- 2–4 sentences
{% /table %}

## Step 1: Write a title people search for

Engineers search for standard titles. "Rockstar Ninja Developer" and "Code Wizard" don't show up in search and look unserious.

**Good titles:**

- Senior Backend Engineer (Go)
- Frontend Engineer, React and TypeScript
- Founding Full-Stack Engineer
- Staff Platform Engineer, Kubernetes
- Machine Learning Engineer, Search

Put the main language or area in the title when it's essential. Include the level (Junior, Senior, Staff) so people at the wrong level don't apply.

## Step 2: Lead with the work, not the company

Most posts open with three paragraphs about the company. Engineers want to know what they'll build.

**Weak opening:**

> We are a fast-growing, innovative company revolutionising the future of logistics with cutting-edge technology.

**Strong opening:**

> You'll own the routing engine that plans 40,000 deliveries a day across six cities. Your first project is cutting route planning time from 20 minutes to under 2, so dispatchers can re-plan in real time when a driver calls in sick.

## Step 3: Separate real requirements from wishes

Long requirement lists put off qualified people, and people who don't see themselves in every bullet often don't apply, even when they'd do well. For each requirement, ask: **would we reject a great candidate who didn't have this?** If not, move it to "nice to have".

**Before:**

- 7+ years of experience
- Expert in Go, Rust, Python and Java
- Experience with Kafka, Kubernetes, Terraform, AWS, GCP and Azure
- Computer science degree from a top university

**After:**

- You've built and run backend services in production, ideally in Go.
- You're comfortable with SQL databases and designing schemas.
- You've owned something end to end: design, build, deploy and on-call.
- You write clearly, because we work async across time zones.

Describe skills and outcomes rather than years and degrees where you can. "Years of experience" is a weak proxy for ability.

## Step 4: Show the tech stack

A one-line stack helps engineers decide in seconds:

> **Stack:** Go, PostgreSQL, Redis, Kafka, Kubernetes on GCP, React and TypeScript on the frontend.

Say which parts the person will work in most.

## Step 5: Publish the salary range

Pay transparency is now required by law in many places, and posts with salaries tend to get more relevant applicants. A range also stops you from spending three rounds with someone whose expectations are far outside your budget.

- Give a realistic range, not "₹5–50 LPA".
- Say whether equity is included.
- Mention any bonus or major benefits.

## Step 6: Be clear about location and work model

Ambiguity here causes many wasted interviews. State:

- Remote, hybrid (how many days, which office) or on-site.
- Countries or time zones you can hire in.
- Required overlap hours, if any.
- Whether you sponsor visas.

## Step 7: Explain the interview process

Strong candidates often have several processes running. Telling them yours up front builds trust:

1. 30-minute intro call with the hiring manager.
2. Short AI screening interview about your experience (15 minutes).
3. Take-home exercise (2 hours, paid) or a pair-programming session.
4. System design conversation.
5. Founder chat and references.

Our guide to [choosing a technical assessment](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/take-home-assignment-vs-live-coding-vs-pair-programming-choosing-a-technical-assessment) helps with step 3.

## Software engineer job description template

Copy and adapt:

```
Title: Senior Backend Engineer (Go)

About the role
You'll own [system] used by [who/how many]. Your first project is [specific
project and goal].

What you'll do
- Design, build and run [services] in Go
- Work with product and design to ship [feature area]
- Improve reliability and performance of [system]
- Review code and help shape our engineering practices
- Join an on-call rotation (one week in six)

What we're looking for
- You've built and run backend services in production
- You're comfortable with PostgreSQL and schema design
- You've owned features end to end
- You communicate clearly in writing

Nice to have
- Experience with Kafka or other event streaming
- Payments or fintech experience

Stack
Go, PostgreSQL, Redis, Kafka, Kubernetes on GCP

Compensation
₹35–45 LPA base + ESOPs

Location
Remote within India, with 4 hours of overlap with IST 11:00–15:00

Interview process
1. Intro call (30 min)
2. AI screening interview (15 min)
3. Pair programming (60 min)
4. System design (60 min)
5. Founder chat

About us
[Stage, product, customers, team size in 2–3 sentences]
```

## Add a few application questions

Two or three good questions reveal more than a cover letter:

- "Tell us about a backend system you've owned in production. What broke, and what did you change?"
- "Which part of this role are you most excited about, and why?"

Typed questions (yes/no, numbers, choices) are useful for hard requirements like work authorisation or notice period. See our guide to [knockout questions](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/knockout-questions-how-to-screen-out-unqualified-applicants-without-losing-good-ones).

## Check for biased language

Some wording quietly narrows who applies:

- Avoid "young", "digital native", "recent graduate" (unless it's a graduate role).
- Replace "rockstar", "ninja" and "aggressive" with plain descriptions of the work.
- Don't require a degree unless the job truly needs one.
- Avoid "native English speaker"; say "fluent written English" if that's what you need.

On GetHired, application question text is checked when you save it, and questions about protected characteristics are refused. Read more about [fair AI hiring](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/fair-ai-hiring-how-gethired-keeps-bias-out-of-candidate-screening).

## Frequently asked questions

### How long should a software engineer job description be?

Usually 300 to 700 words. Long enough to be specific, short enough to read on a phone.

### Should I include years of experience?

Use it sparingly, as a guide rather than a hard rule. Describing the scope of work ("you've led a project across three teams") is more accurate.

### Should a job post include salary?

Yes, where you can. It's legally required in many places, and it saves time for both sides.

### How many requirements should a job post have?

Three to six true requirements, plus a short nice-to-have list. More than that discourages good candidates.

### Can AI write a job description for me?

Yes. On GetHired, the [recruiter agent](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/meet-the-gethired-recruiter-agent-hire-tech-talent-from-one-chat) asks you about the role and drafts the post, including salary, skills and application questions, for you to review before publishing.

